1	//! Utilities for implementing the `readdir` operation on a directory inode.
2	//!
3	//! `readdir` is conceptually simple ("just call ListObjectsV2") but has some subtleties that make
4	//! it complicated:
5	//!
6	//! 1. It's possible for a directory to contain both a subdirectory and a file of the same name, in
7	//!    which case we need to implement shadowing in the right way.
8	//! 2. A directory can also contain local files/subdirectories, which we want to have appear in the
9	//!    readdir stream, but be shadowed by remote files/subdirectories.
10	//! 3. ListObjectsV2 returns common prefixes including the trailing '/', which messes up ordering
11	//!    of entries in the readdir stream. For example, `a-/` < `a/`, but `a-` > `a` in lexicographic
12	//!    order, so we need to re-sort the common prefixes rather than just streaming them directly.
13	//! 4. We want to do large ListObjectsV2 calls (i.e. with a large page size), but `readdir` calls
14	//!    typically are much smaller, so we need to hold onto ListObjectsV2 results for a while. But we
15	//!    don't want them to expire while we're holding onto them.
16	//! 5. FUSE's `readdir` design makes it hard to know in advance exactly how many entries we'll be
17	//!    able to return in a single request (fixed-size buffer but names are variable size), so we
18	//!    need to be able to "peek" the next entry in the stream in case it won't fit.
19	//!
20	//! This module tries to decouple each of these requirements by building a hierarchy of iterators
21	//! to implement the `readdir` stream:
22	//!
23	//! * [ReaddirHandle] is the top-level iterator, and the only public struct in this module. Its
24	//!   results can be directly returned to `readdir`. It takes results from [ReaddirIter] and creates
25	//!   inodes for them, achieving point 4. It also has a [ReaddirHandle::readd] method to handle
26	//!   point 5.
27	//! * [ReaddirIter] is an iterator over [ReaddirEntry]s, which are entries that may not yet have
28	//!   inodes created for them. [ReaddirIter] merges together two streams, [RemoteIter] and
29	//!   [LocalIter], to handle point 2. While merging, [ReaddirIter] also deduplicates the entries it
30	//!   returns to handle point 1.
